var TOPICS = [
  {id:"start", n:"00", t:"How this page works", panel:"p-start",
   ctx:"Orientation. The unifying framework: every instrument becomes a weight vector w over risk factors, then mu_P = w'mu, sigma_P^2 = w'Omega*w, VaR_alpha = mu_P + z_alpha*sigma_P. Table of how each lecture builds w: stock = portfolio share; ZCB = -D_mod * share; coupon bond = (PV_t/B)*(-t) per cash flow; option = [Delta*S/Op ; Rho/Op]; forward = long call + short put combined; futures = [S0 ; T*S0] in money. Two conventions that differ from most textbooks: VaR IS REPORTED NEGATIVE, and the mean mu is kept rather than set to zero. The class workbooks use legacy Excel names NORMSINV, NORMSDIST, STDEV and COVAR. Tiers e/m/d, 80% mastery to mark a tail measured, thirteen errata flagged across the page."},

  {id:"cram", n:"GO", t:"Exam day", panel:"p-cram",
   ctx:"Exam-day panel: the rest of the page sorted by how much time is left, plus the things worth checking under pressure. A revision planner with 20 / 45 / 90 / 180-minute plans, each step linking to the exact section it names. A question router mapping the words in a question to the risk factors and the weight vector: mu/sigma given -> w = 1; money in several assets -> w_i = value_i/W0 with Omega_ij = rho_ij sigma_i sigma_j; T-bill -> w = -D_mod; coupon bond -> w_t = (PV_t/B)(-t); option -> [Delta S/Op ; Rho/Op]; equivalent asset / binomial -> [Delta S/Op ; -(B/Op)T/(1+y)]; forward -> [S/V_F ; TKe^(-yT)/V_F]; futures on an asset -> [S0 ; T S0] in money; ZCB futures -> [-(T+N)B0 ; +T B0]; coupon-bond futures -> one row per post-delivery flow plus a financing row carrying T; a mix -> add adjusted weights factor by factor; discrete loss outcomes -> sort and cumulate; assets/debt -> PD = N((-E0/A0 - mu_A)/sigma_A). The five checks: is VaR negative, do sigma/y/T share one time unit, is w in the same order as Omega, does w_rS - w_dy/T = 1, do the component VaRs sum to the portfolio VaR. The z values (-2.326348, -1.959964, -1.644854, -1.281552) and ES multipliers (2.665214, 2.337803, 2.062713). A weak-spot list built from the student's own wrong answers, and the keyboard shortcuts. Nothing new is taught here."},

  {id:"fi", n:"01", t:"Financial institutions & risk", panel:"p-fi",
   ctx:"Lecture 1, the shortest deck. FI types: banks, securities and investment companies, insurance companies, others. Value maximisation: Assets = Debt + Equity, management and operations add value. Activity-to-risk mapping: business strategy -> strategic risk; business process -> operational risk; lending -> credit risk; security trading -> MARKET RISK; selling insurance -> insurance risk; managing cash flows against obligations -> liquidity risk. Full taxonomy adds concentration, enterprise-wide and group risk. Market risk also called price risk or interest rate risk. Risk is defined as the possibility the FI makes mistakes in its operations leading to financial OR non-financial loss. Bankruptcy: at t=1 the FI is bankrupt if assets < debt, equivalently if the loss exceeds equity, equivalently if r_A < -E0/A0. So PD = NORMSDIST((-E0/A0 - mu_A)/sigma_A) and required equity ratio = -(mu_A + NORMSINV(PD)*sigma_A). Deck's PD table by rating with z = NORMSINV(PD) and capital at sigma_A = 10%, mu = 0: AAA 0.014038% z=-3.632434 cap 36.32%; AA 0.020902% z=-3.528425 cap 35.28%; A 0.123945% z=-3.025905 cap 30.26%; BBB 0.997917% z=-2.327130 cap 23.27%; BB 2.488875% z=-1.961871 cap 19.62%; B 4.611472% z=-1.683753 cap 16.84%; CCC 30.848573% z=-0.500147 cap 5.00%. Two observations: BBB's PD is almost exactly 1% so its z is almost exactly the 99% VaR multiplier; and capital is very non-linear in rating (BB to AAA costs 16.71 points, B to BB only 2.78). Regulation = the rules and framework; supervision = monitoring, inspection and enforcement of them. International bodies: BIS (banks, hosts the Basel Committee), IOSCO (securities), IAIS (insurance). Ends on the three-pillar approach and risk-based supervision. Link to the rest of the course: Pillar 1 market risk capital comes from a VaR or ES model, and the BIS 250-business-day minimum is why every class workbook has 250 rows."},

  {id:"mr", n:"02", t:"Market risk, VaR & ES", panel:"p-mr",
   ctx:"Lecture 2, the core. Returns: discrete r = (P1+D-P0)/P0, continuous r = LN((P1+D)/P0). Price and return are LINEARLY related, r = -1 + D/P0 + (1/P0)P1, so the linear-transformation rule applies: if y = a + b*r and r~N(mu,sigma) then mu_y = a + b*mu and sigma_y^2 = b^2*sigma^2. That single rule licenses every weight vector in the course. VaR = the possible maximum loss over a horizon at a confidence level; at 99% confidence the chance of losing more than the VaR is one in a hundred. z = (x-mu)/sigma, r_alpha = mu + z_alpha*sigma, VaR = (mu + z_alpha*sigma)*W0. Deck z table: 1% -> -2.33, 2.5% -> -1.96, 5% -> -1.64, 10% -> -1.28; Excel returns -2.326348, -1.959964, -1.644854, -1.281552. WORKED EXAMPLE r~N(0.02,0.05): r_0.01 = 0.02 + (-2.33)(0.05) = -0.0965 (deck) or -0.096317 at full precision; r_0.05 = -0.062243; P(any loss) = NORMSDIST((0-0.02)/0.05) = NORMSDIST(-0.4) = 0.3446. Estimation: mu-hat = (1/T)sum(r), sigma-hat^2 = (1/(T-1))sum((r-mu)^2) = STDEV^2. BIS requires at least 250 business days; McNeil and Frey (2000) suggest 1,000. Advantages of VaR: captures risk in a single number, easy to understand, asks 'how bad can things get'. t-test of H0: mu = 0 uses t = mu-hat*SQRT(T)/sigma-hat; deck example 10 obs, mean 0.0289, variance 0.002106 gives t = SQRT(10)*0.0289/0.045891 = 1.9914, below the critical value either way (2.2622 at 9 df, deck uses 2.31 at 8 df). Expected shortfall = expected loss given the loss exceeds VaR, also called C-VaR or tail loss; two portfolios with the same VaR can have very different ES. ES = mu + sigma*EXP(-z^2/2)/(SQRT(2*PI())*alpha); the multiplier is always positive so when mu < 0 write ES = mu - sigma*(...) to match the VaR sign. Multipliers: 2.665214 at 1%, 2.337803 at 2.5%, 2.062713 at 5%. Coherent risk measure properties: monotonicity, translation invariance, homogeneity, subadditivity. VaR satisfies the first three but NOT subadditivity; ES satisfies all four. HULL 11.5/11.7 COUNTEREXAMPLE: two independent projects, each losing $1m with probability 0.98 and $10m with probability 0.02. Each has 97.5% VaR = $1m (since 0.98 >= 0.975) and ES = (0.02*10 + 0.005*1)/0.025 = $8.2m. The portfolio has outcomes $2m at 0.9604, $11m at 0.0392, $20m at 0.0004; since 0.9604 < 0.975 <= 0.9996 the 97.5% VaR is $11m, and ES = (0.0004*20 + 0.0246*11)/0.025 = $11.144m. So VaR: 1+1 = 2 against 11 merged, violated by 9m. ES: 8.2+8.2 = 16.4 against 11.144, holds. Horizon: VaR_T = VaR_1*SQRT(T) and ES_T = ES_1*SQRT(T), but ONLY if losses are iid with ZERO MEAN; since this course keeps mu, use mu*T + z*sigma*SQRT(T). SET index class data, 250 days: mu = -0.0459693%/day, sigma = 0.8359212%/day, sigma annual = 13.269837%. VaR: 1-day 95% -1.420937%, 99% -1.990613%; 10-day 95% -4.807723%, 99% -6.609195%; 252-day 95% -33.411201%, 99% -42.454519%. Volatility: plain SD weights a 250-day-old return as heavily as yesterday's. EWMA sigma_n^2 = lambda*sigma_(n-1)^2 + (1-lambda)*u_(n-1)^2; substituting repeatedly shows weights start at 1-lambda and decline at rate lambda. Attractions: little data to store (only the current variance and the latest return) and it tracks volatility changes. lambda = 0.94 is the popular choice, giving an effective window of 1/(1-lambda) = 16.67 days and a variance half-life of 11.20 days; lambda = 0.97 gives 33.33 days."},

  {id:"fix", n:"03", t:"VaR for fixed income", panel:"p-fix",
   ctx:"Lecture 3. Bond X has 5 years left, Bond Y has 2 - you cannot estimate either from a price history, because time to maturity shrinks daily so yesterday's price belongs to a different instrument. Model the YIELD instead; the deck's alternative return series is the Zero Rate Return (ZRR) index, a price index of a constant-maturity zero. Pricing: B0 = F_T/(1+y_T)^T or approximately EXP(-y_T*T)*F_T; a coupon bond discounts EACH cash flow at its OWN spot rate. Spot rate = the current rate on a zero maturing at that time. Deck distinguishes the yield curve from the zero yield curve (both ThaiBMA); bond pricing needs the ZERO curve. Taylor: dB = -T*B0*dy_T for a continuous zero, so r_B = dB/B0 = -T*dy_T and VaR = z*T*sigma(dy_T) - the bond price cancels entirely, and for a continuous zero duration IS maturity. Discrete: D_mac = sum(t*w_t), D_mod = D_mac/(1+y), dB/B = -D_mod*dy, mu_B = -D_mod*mu_y, sigma_B^2 = D_mod^2*sigma_y^2, VaR = -D_mod*mu_dy + z*D_mod*sigma_dy. T-BILL EXAMPLE: 1-year ZCB, THB 1,000,000 par, yield 3%, semiannual. B = 1,000,000/1.015^2 = 970,661.7486. D_mac = 2 periods (all PV at period 2), D_mod = 2/1.015 = 1.9704433. Ten dy observations from eleven rates: AVERAGE = -0.000175, STDEV = 0.0008895223. mu_B = -1.9704433*(-0.000175) = 0.00034483 (POSITIVE, because yields fell on average), sigma_B = 1.9704433*0.0008895223 = 0.00175275. VaR_1% = 0.00034483 - 2.326348*0.00175275 = -0.00373269, times 970,661.75 = -3,623.18. The deck labels the two sigmas as sigma^2 - they are standard deviations. Coupon bond as a portfolio of zeros: r_B = sum(w_t*r_Bt) with w_t = (1/B0)*CF_t/(1+y_t)^t and r_Bt = -D_mod_t*dy_t, so the ADJUSTED weight multiplying dy_t is w_t*(-duration_t). Covariances collapse: Var(r_Bi) = i^2*Var(dy_i) and Cov(r_Bi,r_Bj) = (-i)(-j)Cov(dy_i,dy_j), so durations fold into w and Omega stays the raw yield-change matrix. Matrix form with the deck's own Excel note: 1. TRANSPOSE(Array) 2. MMULT(Array1,Array2) then Ctrl+Shift+Enter. FIVE-YEAR COUPON BOND, C=3%, FV=100, Thai zero curve: spots 1.510592, 1.621256, 1.777315, 1.910292, 2.326193; PVs 2.9553566, 2.9050401, 2.8455635, 2.7813079, 91.8127808; B = 103.3000490; weights 2.860944%, 2.812235%, 2.754658%, 2.692456%, 88.879707%; adjusted weights -0.0286094, -0.0562447, -0.0826398, -0.1076982, -4.4439853 summing to -4.7191775 = minus the Macaulay duration of 4.7192 years. mu_P = 2.22635e-05, sigma_P^2 = 2.3251e-06, sigma_P = 0.15248%. VaR_1% = -0.35251% and VaR with zero mean = -0.35473%, so because mu is POSITIVE here dropping the mean makes VaR MORE negative - the zero-mean version is the conservative one. On the 103.30 price that is -0.3642 per 100 face. 88.88% of the bond's value sits in the final cash flow. PORTFOLIO OF BASIC ASSETS: bond T=2, C=3% annual, FV=1000, priced CONTINUOUSLY with EXP(-y*t), plus a stock. PVs 29.5502 and 997.1369, bond price 1026.6871, stock 1381.41, W0 = 2408.0971. Weights 1.22712%, 41.40767%, 57.36521%; adjusted weights -0.0122712, -0.8281534, +0.5736521. mu_P = -0.02636%, sigma_P = 0.61286%. VaR(1%,1d) = -1.45208%, VaR(5%,1d) = -1.03442%, VaR(1%,3d) = -2.54849% computed as mu*3 + z*sigma*SQRT(3). The 5% slide prints (-1.96) but the workbook uses NORMSINV(0.05) = -1.644854. Marginal VaR = dVaR/dx_i = (z/sigma_P)*(Omega*w)_i; component VaR = x_i*marginal. Three properties: component VaR is approximately incremental VaR, the components SUM EXACTLY to total VaR by Euler's theorem, and it is therefore a sensible allocation. Incremental VaR is a different number: portfolio VaR minus the VaR of the book without that position."},

  {id:"opt", n:"04", t:"VaR for options", panel:"p-opt",
   ctx:"Lecture 4. Call = right to buy, put = right to sell; European exercisable only at expiry, American any time. Payoffs: long call Max(S_T-K,0), short call Min(K-S_T,0), long put Max(K-S_T,0), short put Min(S_T-K,0). GEARING is the deck's framing of option risk: a call costs far less than the share, so a small percentage move in the asset gives a much larger percentage move in the option, on the upside AND the downside. Two determinants of the option price matter: the underlying price and the interest rate, so market risk = price risk of the underlying + interest rate risk. Treat the option as a portfolio of stock and bond. Stock price lognormal: dS/S = mu*dt + sigma*dz, r_S ~ N(mu - sigma^2/2, sigma^2). BSM: C = S0*N(d1) - K*EXP(-y*T)*N(d2), P = K*EXP(-y*T)*N(-d2) - S0*N(-d1), d1 = (LN(S0/K) + (y + sigma^2/2)T)/(sigma*SQRT(T)), d2 = d1 - sigma*SQRT(T), N(x) = NORMSDIST(x). DECK EXAMPLE S=100, sigma=5%/month, y=1%/month, T=12 months, X=80: d1 = 2.067743 (deck 2.07), N(d1) = 0.980668 (0.98), d2 = 1.894538 (1.89), N(d2) = 0.970923 (0.97), C = 29.176296 (29.17), P = 0.129900 (0.13). Note sigma and y are per MONTH with T in MONTHS - they must share a unit. Only two Greeks matter: Delta = dOp/dS = N(d1) for a call, -N(-d1) for a put; Rho = dOp/dy = T*K*EXP(-y*T)*N(d2) for a call, -T*K*EXP(-y*T)*N(-d2) for a put. First-order Taylor: dOp = Delta*dS + Rho*dy, so r_Op = Delta*(S/Op)*r_S + Rho*(1/Op)*dy, and those two coefficients ARE the weight vector. Omega is the 2x2 matrix of r_S and dy. MAIN EXAMPLE: ATM call and put on stock A, T=1, 99% CL. S0 = K = 1382.41, one-year spot y = 1.510592%, sigma_S = 0.8359212%/day annualising to 13.269837% via STDEV*SQRT(252). d1 = 0.1801857, d2 = 0.0474873, N(d1) = 0.5714966, N(d2) = 0.5189376, C = 83.4134316, P = 62.6877908, Rho_call = 706.629194, Rho_put = -655.055165. Weight vectors, dy FIRST then r_S: w_C = [8.4714078 ; 9.4714078], w_P = [-10.4494856 ; -9.4494856]. KEY IDENTITY: w_rS - w_dy/T = 1 for both, because Rho/T = K*e^(-yT)*N(d2) and C = S*N(d1) - K*e^(-yT)*N(d2), so dividing by C gives C/C = 1. With T = 1 (every class example) the two weights differ by exactly 1; for any other T the plain difference is NOT 1 - check w_rS - w_dy/T instead. mu_C = -0.4524017%, sigma_C = 7.9249710%, VaR_C = -18.888641%; mu_P = +0.4553653%, sigma_P = 7.9086722%, VaR_P = -17.942957%. Against the underlying's -1.99% that is roughly NINE TIMES the risk - gearing quantified. The class Omega has Var(dy) = 1.8796e-08, Var(r_S) = 6.98764e-05, Cov = 6.6829e-08, a correlation of only 0.0583. BUILDING BLOCK / LEGO approach: two blocks, the underlying and a ZCB. One-step binomial gives two equations in two unknowns: C1u = Delta*S1u + B(1+y) and C1d = Delta*S1d + B(1+y), so Delta = (C1u-C1d)/(S(u-d)) and B = (C1d(1+u) - C1u(1+d))/((1+y)(u-d)), with u = EXP(sigma)-1 and d = EXP(-sigma)-1, and p = (y-d)/(u-d). Then r_C = (Delta*S/C)r_S + (B/C)r_B where r_B = -(T/(1+y))dy, so w_C' = [Delta*S/C , -(B/C)(T/(1+y))]. If the bond is priced continuously the duration is T instead of T/(1+y). DECK BINOMIAL EXAMPLE S0=K=1000, sigma=10%, y=3%, T=1: u = 10.5171%, d = -9.5163%, S1u = 1105.17, C1u = 105.17, S1d = 904.84, C1d = 0, Delta = 0.524979, Delta*S = 524.98, B = -461.19 (recomputed -461.1853), C = 63.79 (63.7939), stock weight 8.23, bond weight -7.23, w' = [8.23 ; 7.02] after multiplying by T/(1+y) = 1/1.03. The raw block weights 8.23 and -7.23 sum to exactly 1 because Delta*S + B = C (true for any T). A one-step binomial is crude: on the additional-questions put it gives 0.7864 against Black-Scholes 3.5367, a factor of 4.5, yet the two VaRs are -10.7423% and -10.7474%, within 0.005 points - the weight vector is far more robust than the price. NUMERICAL GREEKS by central difference: dy/dx = (y(x+dx) - y(x-dx))/(2*dx). Class sheet uses dS = 1 and dy = 0.0001: numerical delta 0.5714966 matches analytical exactly, numerical rho 706.6292 matches too. The deck's sanity check: for y = 2x+1 at x=10 with dx=0.1 the central difference gives exactly 2, because for a LINEAR function it is exact at any step size. Digital cash-or-nothing options pay $1 in the money: C = EXP(-y*T)*N(d2), P = EXP(-y*T)*N(-d2). RISK MANAGEMENT, three levers. (1) Reduce exposure: 10 shares at THB 70 with r~N(10%,30%) gives VaR_1% = 10% - 2.33*30% = -59.90%; sell 5 and hold THB 350 cash, so w = [50%,50%], mu = 5%, sigma = 15% (cash has ZERO risk so its Omega row is zeros), VaR = -29.95%. (2) Delta hedge: hold -Delta shares, so dPortfolio = dOp - Delta*dS = 0; the risk from dy is much smaller than from dS. Rho hedge ratio = Rho/(T*EXP(-y_T*T)) held in a ZCB with FV 1 and maturity T. (3) Diversification: mu_A = mu_B = 12%, sigma = 25% each, rho = 0.55; w = [100%,0%] gives VaR = -46.25%, w = [50%,50%] gives sigma_P = SQRT(2*0.5^2*0.25^2 + 2*0.55*0.5*0.5*0.25^2) = 22.0085% and VaR = -39.28%. The deck's summary: portfolio return is the weighted average; cash has zero risk; assets with the SAME source of risk can be offset exactly (hedging); assets with non-zero correlation can only be partly offset (diversification)."},

  {id:"fut", n:"05", t:"VaR for forwards & futures", panel:"p-fut",
   ctx:"Lecture 5. Futures = agreement to buy or sell at a future time T for a set price, traded on an exchange; forwards are the OTC equivalent. Long = agree to buy. TWO DECOMPOSITIONS: a forward is equivalent to LONG ONE CALL + SHORT ONE PUT both struck at 0F_T (proved by a payoff table: in both states the portfolio pays S_T - 0F_T); a futures is equivalent to LONG THE ASSET + BORROWING its spot price (cost of carry). Equivalent assets have the same payoff in every state so the same price and the same risk; a dominant asset pays more in one state and no less in the others, which is what makes an arbitrage argument work. FORWARD EXAMPLE: T=1, 0F_T = THB 120, S0 = 118, sigma = 30% p.a., y_1Y = 2%. Call 14.236219 (deck 14.24), Delta_C 0.563813 (0.56), Rho_C 52.293678 (DECK SAYS 25.29 - WRONG, looks like a transposition), Put 13.860060 (13.86), Delta_P -0.436187 (-0.44), Rho_P -65.330163 (-65.33), V_F = C - P = 0.376159 (0.38). Risk: r_VF = [(C/V_F)Delta_C(S/C) - (P/V_F)Delta_P(S/P)]r_S + [(C/V_F)Rho_C(1/C) - (P/V_F)Rho_P(1/P)]dy, and the C's and P's cancel to give w_rS = (Delta_C - Delta_P)S/V_F = S/V_F because Delta_C - Delta_P = 1 exactly, and w_dy = (Rho_C - Rho_P)/V_F = T*K*e^(-yT)/V_F because Rho_C - Rho_P = T*K*e^(-yT)[N(d2)+N(-d2)]. Correct weights [313.70 ; 312.70], differing by exactly 1 because T = 1 (in general w_rS - w_dy/T = 1); the deck reports [310.52 ; 238.47] and the 238.47 is wrong because of the Rho typo (with the correct Rho and the deck's rounded V_F = 0.38 it would be 309.54). COST OF CARRY: 0F_T = S0*EXP(y_T*T); example S=100, T=6 months, y=3% p.a. gives 100*EXP(0.03/12*6) = 101.5113. FUTURES DECOMPOSITION: Fu = +1*S - 1*B with B_T = S0*e^(y_T*T) and B0 = S0, so dB = -T*B0*dy_T = -T*S0*dy_T and dFu = dS - dB = dS + T*S0*dy_T. Two minus signs make a plus: a long futures GAINS when rates rise, because it is short a bond. Writing dS = S0*r_S gives dFu = S0*r_S + T*S0*dy_T so w = [S0 ; T*S0] = [100 ; 50] for the example - IN MONEY. The deck asks 'can we find % return for a futures contract?' and the answer is NO, because its initial value is zero, so futures VaR is always in money units, never a percentage. ZCB FUTURES: underlying ZCB has FV 1 and TTM T+N, contract life T. 0F_T = EXP(-y_(T+N)(T+N) + y_T*T). dFu = -(T+N)B0*dy_(T+N) + T*B0*dy_T so w = [-(T+N) ; +T]*B0. Example T=1, N=0.5, y_T = 5%, y_(T+N) = 5.2%: 0F_T = EXP(-0.052*1.5 + 0.05) = 0.972388 (deck 0.9724), B0 = EXP(-0.052*1.5) = 0.924964 (0.9250), w = [-1.387447 ; +0.924964]. They do not cancel: the sum is -0.4624 = -N*B0, so a long bond-futures position is net long duration by exactly the extra N periods of life - and that is the source of basis risk. COUPON BOND FUTURES: only POST-DELIVERY cash flows count. 0F_T = {sum of CF*EXP(-y_(T+k)(T+k))}*EXP(y_T*T), equivalently discount at the forward rates (T)f(K) = [y_(T+K)(T+K) - y_T*T]/K. DECK EXAMPLE T=1 year, FV=100, C=4% p.a. semiannual, underlying has 2 years left at delivery, so periods are half-years, delivery is period 2 and coupons fall at periods 3,4,5,6 with spots 2.00%, 2.50%, 3.00%, 3.50% p.a.: PVs 1.940891, 1.902459, 1.855487, 91.833101 summing to 97.531938, times EXP(0.015/2*2) = 1.015113 gives 0F_T = 99.0059. THE DECK SAYS 119.61 AND 121.42, WHICH ARE IMPOSSIBLE because the undiscounted cash flows total only 108. Risk: w = [-(T+N)X_N*B_N,0 ; -(T+M)X_M*B_M,0 ; +T*(X_N*B_N,0 + X_M*B_M,0)] for factors dy_(T+N), dy_(T+M), dy_T - every underlying cash flow gets its own long-bond row, and ALL of them share ONE financing row which carries a factor of T. Deck example T=2 periods, contract 1 X_N = $2 with N=1, contract 2 X_M = $102 with M=2, y2=5%, y3=7%, y4=8%: B_N = EXP(-0.21) = 0.810584, B_M = EXP(-0.32) = 0.726149, w = [-4.8635 ; -296.2688 ; 151.3767]. THE DECK SAYS 75.68 for the third element, having dropped the coefficient T. PRACTICE PROBLEM (Spot Curves workbook): 5-year G.Bond, FV THB 1,000, C = 6% p.a. annual, continuous discounting off the Thai zero curve. PVs 59.100456, 58.085696, 56.884626, 55.586084, 943.611502, B = 1,173.268365; weights 5.037250%, 4.950760%, 4.848390%, 4.737713%, 80.425888%; adjusted weights summing to -4.5056423 = minus the Macaulay duration. mu = 2.46702e-05, sigma = 0.14171%, VaR_1% = -0.32720% = -3.8389 Baht. Then five ZCB futures with T = 1 year on ZCBs maturing at 2,3,4,5,6 years: futures prices 58.969795, 57.750444, 56.432137, 54.224934, 931.907152, with forward rates 1.73192%, 1.910677%, 2.043525%, 2.530093%, 2.575820% reproducing them exactly as a check. Futures book: mu = 0.0525646, sigma = 1.7048701 (money), VaR_1% = -3.9136 Baht. Can the net risk be eliminated completely? NO - the bond loads on dy1..dy5 while the futures book loads on dy2..dy6 plus dy1 through the shared financing leg, so six factors against five contracts with a different loading pattern. The residual is basis risk."},

  {id:"xl", n:"XL", t:"The spreadsheet layer", panel:"p-xl",
   ctx:"Cross-cutting Excel reference. The eight-step skeleton every class workbook shares: raw data; factor changes (=(B5-B4)/100 for dy, =LN(E5/E4) for returns); means (=AVERAGE); Omega (=varcovar, a custom VBA function); the instrument (price, PV weights, durations or Greeks); w; mu_P and sigma_P as two array formulas; VaR. Steps 1-4 and 7-8 are identical everywhere - only 5 and 6 change between lectures. Legacy-to-modern map: NORMSINV = NORM.S.INV (identical); NORMSDIST(z) = NORM.S.DIST(z,TRUE) (the modern one NEEDS the TRUE, and with FALSE gives the density); STDEV = STDEV.S (both n-1); STDEVP = STDEV.P (both n); COVAR = COVARIANCE.P, the POPULATION version dividing by n, NOT COVARIANCE.S. The class sheets pair STDEV^2 (n-1) on the diagonal with COVAR (n) off it, making off-diagonals 0.4% too small at n=250 - Cov(dy,r_S) = 6.682909e-08 where the consistent sample value is 6.709748e-08; the VaR effect is in the fifth decimal place. NORMSDIST takes no second argument, so there is no legacy way to get the density, which is why the calculators build it by hand. varcovar is a VBA UDF supplied with the course, not standard Excel; reproduce it with Data Analysis > Covariance (static, population divisor), a grid of COVARIANCE.S, or =MMULT(TRANSPOSE(dev),dev)/(n-1) on mean-centred data. Array formulas: Ctrl+Shift+Enter is required in Excel 2019 and earlier; without it an array formula returns only the TOP-LEFT element with no error, and the classic symptom is a portfolio sigma that is far too small. Select the whole output range before entering. Microsoft 365 spills automatically and shows #SPILL! if blocked. Functions by job: NORMSINV/NORMSDIST for quantiles and the CDF; AVERAGE/STDEV/COVAR/CORREL for mu and Omega; LN/EXP for log returns and continuous discounting; SQRT; MMULT/TRANSPOSE for the quadratic form; SUMPRODUCT for Macaulay duration and as a TRANSPOSE-free alternative =SUMPRODUCT(w,MMULT(Om,w)); SUM/INDEX; MAX for binomial payoffs; PI() for the density; Goal Seek and Solver for implied volatility and for the minimum-risk hedge. NOTABLY ABSENT: no PRICE, YIELD, DURATION or MDURATION anywhere in the course, because those take a single yield to maturity and real dates, while this course discounts every cash flow at its own spot rate over integer periods. Do not reach for MDURATION in an exam here - build the column. Trap list: -1.96 for a 95% VaR (it belongs to 2.5%); forgetting the /100 on dy; sigma labelled as sigma^2; w order not matching Omega order (the option sheet puts dy FIRST); missing Ctrl+Shift+Enter; mixing continuous PV with discrete duration; 22/7 for pi; scaling a whole VaR by SQRT(T); expecting a percentage return on a futures contract; STDEV^2 beside COVAR; annual sigma with T in months; LOG instead of LN; including pre-delivery coupons in a futures price; and dropping the T from the financing row of a coupon-bond-futures weight vector."},

  {id:"fx", n:"FX", t:"Formulas & functions", panel:"p-fx",
   ctx:"Formula bank: every formula of the course grouped by returns and statistics, VaR and ES of one return, portfolios in matrix form, fixed income, options, and forwards/futures/hedging, each with when to use it, the Excel that computes it, the solver sheet, and its trap. Also six multi-step chains (portfolio from money and correlations; raw data to a mixed portfolio; coupon bond VaR; option VaR both ways plus hedge; futures and forwards; minimum-variance hedge with futures), a dictionary of the Excel functions used (NORMSINV, NORMSDIST, EXP, LN, SQRT, PI, AVERAGE, STDEV, VAR, COVAR vs COVARIANCE.S, CORREL, Data Analysis Covariance, MMULT, TRANSPOSE, SUMPRODUCT, MINVERSE, IRR, TINV, TDIST, SKEW, KURT, MAX, SMALL, F4, Goal Seek, Solver), and a line-by-line check of the student's handwritten notes: log vs simple returns, weight x -D_mod vs -t, diversification benefit sign, marginal and component VaR, sigma from price returns and y = latest rate for BSM, binomial d < r < u, call = long stock + short bond, futures replication. The exam is four long multi-step questions."},

  {id:"pset", n:"AS", t:"Assignments", panel:"p-pset",
   ctx:"All three additional questions from Additional question_ans_Market risk.xlsm, worked in full, plus the two practice problems the decks set. Q1: $300,000 gold and $500,000 silver, daily volatilities 1.8% and 1.2%, correlation 0.6 - find the 10-day 97.5% portfolio VaR, the diversification benefit, and the marginal and component VaR of each. Uses =_xlfn.NORM.S.INV(0.025) = -1.9599639845. Weights 0.375 and 0.625; sigma_P = 0.01275 exactly; 1-day VaR = -2.498954% = -$19,991.63; 10-day = -$63,219.09; sum of individual 10-day VaRs = -$70,656.63 so the diversification benefit is $7,437.54. Marginal VaR = (z/sigma_P)*MMULT(VCV,w) = -0.0311288 gold and -0.0213060 silver, or -$24,903.07 and -$17,044.77 on the $800,000 book. Component VaR = marginal x weight = -0.0116733 and -0.0133162, or -$9,338.65 and -$10,652.98, summing to exactly -$19,991.63. Shares 46.71% gold and 53.29% silver. Q2: eleven days of 1Y and 2Y spot rates and prices of stocks A and B; portfolio of 20 one-year ZCBs, 20 two-year ZCBs, 100 shares of A and 100 of B, FV THB 1,000, annual compounding, 1-day 99% VaR. Uses SIMPLE returns for the stocks, =(D29-D28)/D28. Bond values 1000/1.0736 = 931.4456 times 20 = 18,628.91 and 1000/1.0801^2 = 857.1801 times 20 = 17,143.60; stocks 12,800 and 4,200; W0 = 52,772.51. Weights 0.3530041, 0.3248585, 0.2425505, 0.0795869. Modified durations 1/(1+y1) = 0.9314456 and 2/(1+y2) = 1.8516804, stocks 1. Adjusted weights -0.3288041, -0.6015342, +0.2425505, +0.0795869. mu_P = 0.0086619, sigma_P = 0.0186555, VaR = mu + NORMSINV(1-0.99)*sigma = -3.47372% = -THB 1,833.17. Q3: the same data plus a stock index, portfolio of 5 shares of A, 10 of B and one long put on the index with strike 1,050 and T = 1 year, 1-day 99% VaR, done BOTH ways. Option-pricing-model approach: sigma annualised as (Var_daily*252)^0.5 = 0.0731397, y = 0.0736, S = 1,071.37; d1 = 1.3183363, d2 = 1.2451966, N(d1) = 0.90630445, N(d2) = 0.8934702; call 99.4112839, put 3.5366825; Delta_put = -0.09369555, Rho_put = -103.9192839; W0 = 640 + 420 + 3.5367 = 1,063.5367; weights for dy -0.0977110 and for the index -0.0943857, stock A 0.6017658, stock B 0.3949088; mu = 0.0302928, sigma = 0.0592202, VaR = -10.74739%. Building-block approach: u = EXP(sigma)-1 = 0.0758809, d = -0.0705290, S1u = 1,152.6665 with P1u = 0, S1d = 995.8073 with P1d = 54.1927; Delta = -0.3454864, stock value -370.1437, bond value 370.9301, put price 0.7863658 - note the one-step binomial puts it at 0.7864 against Black-Scholes 3.5367; block weights -470.7017 stock and +471.7017 bond, summing to 1; W0 = 1,060.7864; adjusted weights -0.3257029 for dy and -0.3489333 for the index; mu = 0.0304842, sigma = 0.0592808, VaR = -10.74235%. The two approaches agree to within 0.005 percentage points despite the price differing by a factor of 4.5. Practice problems: the plain-vanilla and digital option exercises, and the 5-year 6% G.Bond hedged with ZCB futures."},

  {id:"exam", n:"EX", t:"Exam prep", panel:"p-exam",
   ctx:"A timed self-marking mock drawn from every quiz on the page in a 50/25/25 tier mix at 1.75 minutes a question, with 12/20/40 question options and coverage filters for foundations (lectures 1-2), instruments (3-5), or lectures only. NO EXAM GUIDELINES were supplied with the course files, so the mix and timing are the study kit's defaults and the panel says so - the student should ask for the real tier weighting, time allowed, whether a formula sheet is permitted, and whether lecture 5 is examinable. The panel also carries the A4 formula sheet: the master formula VaR = w'mu + z*SQRT(w'Omega*w); the four z values; the ES formula and its multipliers 2.665214, 2.337803, 2.062713; mean scales with T and volatility with SQRT(T) and sigma_annual = sigma_daily*SQRT(252); duration and the coupon-bond weight; Black-Scholes with d1 and d2 and all four Greeks; the option weight vector with the w_rS - w_dy/T = 1 check; the forward and futures weight vectors with the note that futures weights are in money; ZCB and coupon-bond futures weights with the forward-rate formula; and marginal, component and incremental VaR. Three non-formulas worth the space: VaR is negative in this course, the mean stays in, and sigma/y/T must share one time unit."}
];

/* flashcards: t title, h hint (front), b back */
var FLASH = {
  fi: [
    {t:"Risk, as this course defines it", h:"not just losing money", b:"The possibility that the institution <b>makes mistakes in its operations</b>, and those mistakes lead to <b>financial or non-financial loss</b>. Reputational damage counts even when no money moves."},
    {t:"Activity → risk", h:"six lines worth memorising", b:"Strategy → strategic. Process → operational. Lending → credit. <b>Security trading → market</b>. Insurance policies → insurance. Cash flows against obligations → liquidity."},
    {t:"Market risk's other names", h:"three labels, one thing", b:"<b>Market risk = price risk = interest rate risk.</b> Worth knowing because lectures 3 to 5 measure interest rate risk and call the answer market risk."},
    {t:"Bankruptcy as a tail event", h:"rewrite it as a return", b:"Bankrupt when Ã₁ &lt; D₀, which is the same as <b>r̃<sub>A</sub> &lt; −E₀/A₀</b>. So PD is the left-tail probability of the asset return cut at minus the equity ratio — a VaR statement read backwards."},
    {t:"Required capital", h:"pick a PD, get an equity ratio", b:"<b>E₀/A₀ = −(μ<sub>A</sub> + NORMSINV(PD)·σ<sub>A</sub>)</b>. At σ<sub>A</sub> = 10% and μ = 0: BBB needs 23.27%, AAA needs 36.32%. <b>Very non-linear</b> — BB to AAA costs 16.71 points, B to BB only 2.78."},
    {t:"BBB ≈ 99% confidence", h:"a coincidence worth using", b:"BBB's PD of <b>0.997917%</b> is almost exactly 1%, so its z is almost exactly <b>−2.3263</b> — the 99% VaR multiplier. An investment-grade floor <i>is</i> a 99%-confidence statement."},
    {t:"Regulation vs supervision", h:"writing rules vs checking them", b:"<b>Regulation</b> is the rules, laws and standards — the framework, written once for everyone. <b>Supervision</b> is monitoring, inspection and enforcement — continuous and institution-specific."},
    {t:"Who sets the standards", h:"three bodies, three sectors", b:"<b>BIS</b> for banks (the Basel Committee sits there), <b>IOSCO</b> for securities markets, <b>IAIS</b> for insurance."},
    {t:"Why 250 days", h:"the number in every workbook", b:"<b>BIS requires at least 250 business days</b> of data for a market risk model. That is why every class workbook has 250 rows. McNeil and Frey (2000) suggest 1,000 would be better."}
  ],
  mr: [
    {t:"The linear-transformation rule", h:"the engine of the whole course", b:"If ỹ = a + b·r̃ with r̃ ~ N(μ,σ) then <b>μ<sub>y</sub> = a + bμ</b> and <b>σ<sub>y</sub>² = b²σ²</b>. The constant drops out of the variance. Every weight vector in the course is an application of this."},
    {t:"VaR, stated properly", h:"three things must be named", b:"The possible maximum loss over a <b>horizon</b> at a <b>confidence level</b>, for a given <b>position</b>. \"At 99% confidence the loss is 8% over one year\" means losing more than 8% happens one year in a hundred."},
    {t:"VaR = μ + z<sub>α</sub>σ", h:"and z is negative", b:"Times W₀ for money. <b>The output is negative</b> in this course — that is the lecturer's convention, so keep it and label it. z(1%) = −2.326348, z(5%) = −1.644854."},
    {t:"Where 1.96 belongs", h:"the most expensive two decimals", b:"<b>α = 2.5%, confidence 97.5%.</b> A 95% VaR wants <b>−1.644854</b>. Using −1.96 for 95% overstates the VaR by 19.2%, and one lecture-3 slide does exactly that."},
    {t:"Probability of any loss", h:"same formula, other direction", b:"z = (0 − μ)/σ, then <b>NORMSDIST(z)</b>. For μ = 2%, σ = 5%: NORMSDIST(−0.4) = <b>34.46%</b>. Losing money is ordinary; losing 9.65% is not."},
    {t:"Expected shortfall", h:"the average of the tail", b:"The expected loss <b>given</b> it exceeds VaR. ES = μ + σ·e<sup>−z²/2</sup>/(√(2π)α). The multiplier is <b>2.665214 at 1%</b>. Two portfolios with the same VaR can have very different ES."},
    {t:"The ES sign trap", h:"the deck warns about this explicitly", b:"The multiplier term is <b>always positive</b>. When μ &lt; 0, write <b>ES = μ − σ·e<sup>−z²/2</sup>/(√(2π)α)</b> so the sign matches the VaR convention. Easiest formula in the deck to get backwards."},
    {t:"Coherence", h:"four properties, one failure", b:"Monotonicity, translation invariance, homogeneity, <b>subadditivity</b>. VaR satisfies the first three and fails the fourth; ES satisfies all four. Failing subadditivity means splitting a desk in two could <i>lower</i> measured risk."},
    {t:"Hull's counterexample", h:"two independent projects", b:"Each loses $1m at p = 0.98, $10m at p = 0.02. Individually 97.5% VaR = <b>$1m</b>, ES = <b>$8.2m</b>. Merged: VaR = <b>$11m</b>, ES = <b>$11.144m</b>. VaR rose from 2 to 11 on merging; ES fell from 16.4 to 11.14."},
    {t:"√T scaling's three conditions", h:"the third is the one you forget", b:"Independent, normally distributed, and <b>zero mean</b>. This course keeps μ, so use <b>μT + z·σ√T</b>. The mean scales with <b>T</b>, the volatility with <b>√T</b>."},
    {t:"The t-test on μ", h:"is the mean worth keeping?", b:"<b>t = μ̂√T/σ̂</b>. Deck example: √10 × 0.0289 / 0.045891 = <b>1.9914</b>, below 2.2622 at 9 df, so you cannot reject μ = 0. The course keeps μ anyway."},
    {t:"EWMA", h:"a function Excel does not have", b:"<b>σ<sub>n</sub>² = λσ<sub>n−1</sub>² + (1−λ)u<sub>n−1</sub>²</b>. Weights start at 1−λ and decay at λ. <b>λ = 0.94</b> gives an effective window of <b>16.67 days</b>. Attractions: almost no data to store, and it tracks volatility changes."}
  ],
  fix: [
    {t:"Why not use bond prices", h:"the instrument changes under you", b:"<b>Time to maturity shrinks daily</b>, so yesterday's price belongs to a different bond. Model the <b>yield</b> instead — a fixed point on the curve is comparable across days. Or use the <b>ZRR index</b>."},
    {t:"Spot rate, not yield to maturity", h:"one rate per cash flow", b:"A coupon bond discounts <b>each cash flow at its own spot rate</b>: CF<sub>t</sub>/(1+y<sub>t</sub>)<sup>t</sup>. That is exactly what makes it a portfolio of zeros. The <b>zero</b> yield curve, not the par yield curve."},
    {t:"The Taylor result", h:"the bond price cancels", b:"For a continuous zero, dB = −T·B₀·dy, so <b>r<sub>B</sub> = −T·dy</b> and the price disappears entirely. For a continuously-compounded zero, <b>duration is maturity</b>."},
    {t:"Macaulay vs modified", h:"one factor apart", b:"<b>D<sub>mac</sub> = Σ t·w<sub>t</sub></b>, the PV-weighted average time. <b>D<sub>mod</sub> = D<sub>mac</sub>/(1+y)</b>. Continuous discounting uses <b>−t</b>; discrete uses <b>−t/(1+y<sub>t</sub>)</b>. Pick one and stay with it."},
    {t:"The adjusted weight", h:"the one thing the exam tests", b:"<b>w<sub>t</sub> × (−duration<sub>t</sub>)</b>, where w<sub>t</sub> = PV<sub>t</sub>/W₀. That single product turns a cash flow table into a weight vector. The adjusted weights <b>sum to minus the Macaulay duration</b> — a free check."},
    {t:"Why durations fold into w", h:"and Ω stays raw", b:"Cov(r<sub>Bi</sub>, r<sub>Bj</sub>) = (−i)(−j)·Cov(dy<sub>i</sub>, dy<sub>j</sub>). The durations come out as plain multipliers, so you can put them in <b>w</b> and leave <b>Ω</b> as the raw yield-change matrix."},
    {t:"The zero-coupon sanity check", h:"one line, catches a lot", b:"A zero's <b>Macaulay duration equals its maturity</b>, because all the PV sits at one date. So any coupon bond's duration must be <b>shorter</b> than its maturity. If a function hands you otherwise, something is wrong."},
    {t:"The T-bill answer", h:"know the shape of it", b:"THB 1m par, 3% semiannual: B = 970,661.75, D<sub>mod</sub> = 1.9704433, μ<sub>B</sub> = +0.00034483, σ<sub>B</sub> = 0.00175275, VaR<sub>1%</sub> = −0.373269% = <b>−3,623.18</b>. Note μ<sub>B</sub> is <b>positive</b> because yields fell."},
    {t:"When zero-mean is conservative", h:"check the sign of μ̂ first", b:"On the coupon bond μ̂<sub>P</sub> is <b>positive</b>, so dropping it makes VaR go from −0.35251% to <b>−0.35473%</b> — <i>more</i> negative. Never assume ignoring the mean is the safe choice."},
    {t:"Component VaR adds up", h:"Euler's theorem", b:"CVaR<sub>i</sub> = w<sub>i</sub> × marginal VaR<sub>i</sub>, and <b>the components sum exactly to the portfolio VaR</b>. That makes it the only defensible way to allocate risk to desks, and a free arithmetic check."},
    {t:"Component ≠ incremental", h:"two different questions", b:"<b>Component</b> is the derivative times the position. <b>Incremental</b> is portfolio VaR minus the VaR of the book <i>without</i> that position. On the MS/ATT book: component 1,436,390 against incremental 1,252,264."},
    {t:"Ctrl+Shift+Enter", h:"the deck says it for a reason", b:"Without it, in Excel 2019 and earlier, <b>MMULT returns only the top-left element</b> — no error, just a wrong number. The symptom is a portfolio σ that looks far too small."}
  ],
  opt: [
    {t:"Gearing", h:"why option weights are huge", b:"A call costs far less than the share, so a small percentage move in the asset is a large percentage move in the option — <b>upside and downside equally</b>. That is why the weight vectors come out as 9.47 and −10.45 rather than fractions."},
    {t:"Only two Greeks", h:"this course ignores the rest", b:"<b>Delta = ∂Op/∂S</b> and <b>Rho = ∂Op/∂y</b>. Delta = N(d₁) for a call, −N(−d₁) for a put. Rho = TKe<sup>−yT</sup>N(d₂) for a call, −TKe<sup>−yT</sup>N(−d₂) for a put. Gamma and Vega appear in the calculator but not in the VaR."},
    {t:"The option weight vector", h:"two coefficients from one Taylor line", b:"dOp = Δ·dS + ρ·dy, so r<sub>Op</sub> = Δ(S/Op)r<sub>S</sub> + ρ(1/Op)dy and <b>w = [Δ·S/Op ; ρ/Op]</b>. The class sheet orders it <b>dy first</b> — match Ω's order."},
    {t:"The w_rS − w_dy/T = 1 check", h:"free, exact, and nobody uses it", b:"<b>w<sub>rS</sub> − w<sub>dy</sub>/T = 1</b>, because ρ/T = Ke<sup>−yT</sup>N(d₂) and C = S·N(d₁) − Ke<sup>−yT</sup>N(d₂). Holds for calls, puts and forwards. With <b>T = 1</b> the weights simply differ by 1; for other T divide the dy weight by T first."},
    {t:"Units must match", h:"σ, y and T share one clock", b:"The deck's own example uses <b>σ = 5% per month, y = 1% per month, T = 12 months</b>. Mixing an annual σ with T in months is the fastest way to destroy an option calculation."},
    {t:"The nine-times answer", h:"gearing, measured", b:"On the 1,382.41 example both options have a one-day VaR near <b>−18%</b> against the underlying's <b>−1.99%</b>. The call is riskier than the put almost entirely because μ was negative over the window, not because σ differed."},
    {t:"The LEGO decomposition", h:"two blocks, two equations", b:"Option = <b>stock + bond</b>. In a one-step binomial, two states give two equations: Δ = (C₁u − C₁d)/(S(u−d)) and B = (C₁d(1+u) − C₁u(1+d))/((1+y)(u−d)), with u = e<sup>σ</sup> − 1."},
    {t:"Binomial price vs BSM price", h:"crude price, robust weights", b:"On the assignment put: binomial <b>0.7864</b>, Black-Scholes <b>3.5367</b> — a factor of 4.5. Yet the VaRs are <b>−10.7423%</b> and <b>−10.7474%</b>. <b>The weight vector survives what the price does not.</b>"},
    {t:"Central difference", h:"two revaluations per Greek", b:"<b>(y(x+Δx) − y(x−Δx))/(2Δx)</b>. For a <b>linear</b> function it is exact at any step size — the deck proves it on y = 2x+1. ΔS = 1 on a spot of 1,382 is about 0.07%, a good default."},
    {t:"Delta hedge vs diversification", h:"remove vs dilute", b:"Same source of risk → <b>offset exactly</b>: hold −Δ shares and dPortfolio = 0. Non-zero correlation → <b>only partly offset</b>. <b>Hedging removes risk; diversification dilutes it.</b>"},
    {t:"Cash in Ω", h:"a row of zeros", b:"Cash has <b>zero risk and zero return</b>, so its variance and all its covariances are zero. Selling half a position into cash gives σ<sub>Port</sub> = 0.5σ exactly — a <b>linear</b> lever, unlike diversification."},
    {t:"22/7 is not π", h:"a one-character bug", b:"Three class calculators compute the density as <code>SQRT(2*22/7)</code>, making it — and therefore <b>Vega and Gamma</b> — <b>0.02012% too small</b>. Prices, Delta and Rho use NORMSDIST and are unaffected, so the VaRs are fine."}
  ],
  fut: [
    {t:"A forward is a call minus a put", h:"prove it with a payoff table", b:"Long call + short put, both struck at <sub>0</sub>F<sub>T</sub>, pays <b>S<sub>T</sub> − <sub>0</sub>F<sub>T</sub> in every state</b>. Identical payoffs means identical price and identical risk, so V<sub>F</sub> = C − P."},
    {t:"A futures is asset minus bond", h:"cost of carry", b:"Borrow S₀, buy the asset, hold to T. Net cash flow today is zero, so no arbitrage gives <b><sub>0</sub>F<sub>T</sub> = S₀e<sup>y<sub>T</sub>T</sup></b>."},
    {t:"No percentage return", h:"the deck asks and answers this", b:"A futures contract is <b>worth zero at inception</b>, so there is nothing to divide by. Futures VaR is done entirely in <b>money</b>: w = [S₀ ; T·S₀], and the answer comes out in Baht, not percent."},
    {t:"Long futures gains when rates rise", h:"two minus signs", b:"dB = −T·S₀·dy and dFu = dS − dB, so <b>dFu = dS + T·S₀·dy</b>. A long futures is <b>short a bond</b>, so rising rates help it."},
    {t:"The forward's two weights", h:"they collapse beautifully", b:"<b>w<sub>rS</sub> = S/V<sub>F</sub></b> because Δ<sub>C</sub> − Δ<sub>P</sub> = 1 exactly. <b>w<sub>dy</sub> = TKe<sup>−yT</sup>/V<sub>F</sub></b> because ρ<sub>C</sub> − ρ<sub>P</sub> = TKe<sup>−yT</sup>. So w<sub>rS</sub> − w<sub>dy</sub>/T = 1 — exactly 1 apart when T = 1."},
    {t:"ZCB futures weights", h:"opposite signs, no cancellation", b:"<b>w = [−(T+N), +T] × B₀</b>. They sum to <b>−N·B₀</b>, so a long bond-futures position is <b>net long duration</b> by exactly the extra N periods. <b>N is the source of basis risk.</b>"},
    {t:"Forward rate", h:"two routes, one futures price", b:"<b>(T)f(N) = [y<sub>T+N</sub>(T+N) − y<sub>T</sub>T]/N</b>. Carrying the spot price forward and discounting at the forward rate give the <b>same</b> futures price — use it as a check."},
    {t:"Only post-delivery cash flows", h:"and one impossible-answer test", b:"Coupons paid <b>before</b> delivery never enter <sub>0</sub>F<sub>T</sub>. And the futures price must sit <b>between the PV of the post-delivery flows and their undiscounted sum</b>. That check catches the deck's 121.42 against a cash flow total of 108."},
    {t:"The shared financing row", h:"and the T that gets dropped", b:"Every underlying cash flow gets its own long-bond row, but they <b>all share one financing row</b>: <b>+T × Σ(X<sub>k</sub>B<sub>k</sub>)</b>. The deck's example drops the T and prints 75.68 where 151.3767 is correct."},
    {t:"Why the hedge can't be perfect", h:"count the factors", b:"The 5-year bond loads on dy₁…dy₅. Five ZCB futures load on dy₂…dy₆ <b>plus dy₁</b> through the shared financing leg. <b>Six factors, five contracts, different loadings</b> — minimise the residual, never zero it."},
    {t:"Rho was 52.29, not 25.29", h:"the deck's biggest single error", b:"On S=118, K=120, σ=30%, y=2%, T=1 the class calculator gives <b>Rho(call) = 52.293678</b>. The deck's 25.29 looks like a transposition, and it turns the correct dy weight of <b>312.70</b> into 238.47."}
  ],
  xl: [
    {t:"The eight-step skeleton", h:"every workbook, same shape", b:"Data → factor changes → means → Ω → instrument → <b>w</b> → μ<sub>P</sub> and σ<sub>P</sub> → VaR. <b>Steps 1–4 and 7–8 never change.</b> Only the instrument and the weight column differ between lectures."},
    {t:"COVAR is the population version", h:"the one legacy name that differs", b:"<b>COVAR = COVARIANCE.P</b>, dividing by <b>n</b>. But <b>STDEV = STDEV.S</b>, dividing by <b>n−1</b>. The class sheets pair them, making off-diagonals 0.4% too small at n = 250."},
    {t:"NORMSDIST has no second argument", h:"so there is no legacy density", b:"That is why the class calculators build the density by hand as <code>=EXP(-(d^2)/2)/SQRT(2*PI())</code>. Modern equivalent: <b>NORM.S.DIST(d, FALSE)</b>."},
    {t:"varcovar is not Excel", h:"it is VBA from the course files", b:"A user-defined function, which is why the files are <code>.xlsm</code>. Reproduce it with <b>Data Analysis → Covariance</b>, a grid of <b>COVARIANCE.S</b>, or <code>=MMULT(TRANSPOSE(dev),dev)/(n-1)</code> on mean-centred data."},
    {t:"MMULT takes two arrays", h:"so nest it", b:"<code>=SQRT(MMULT(MMULT(TRANSPOSE(w),Om),w))</code>. Inner product first. Or dodge TRANSPOSE entirely: <code>=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT(w,MMULT(Om,w)))</code>."},
    {t:"σ<sub>P</sub> far too small?", h:"one keystroke", b:"Check <b>Ctrl+Shift+Enter</b>. Without it, older Excel returns the <b>top-left element</b> of the matrix product — w₁²Ω₁₁ instead of wᵀΩw — with no error at all."},
    {t:"No PRICE, no MDURATION", h:"and that is deliberate", b:"Excel's bond functions take a <b>single yield to maturity and real dates</b>. This course discounts <b>every cash flow at its own spot rate</b> over integer periods. Build the column; don't reach for the function."},
    {t:"The /100 on dy", h:"a factor-of-100 error", b:"Spot rates are quoted in percent, so <code>=(B5-B4)/100</code>. Omit the division and every bond VaR is a hundred times too large — and it will not look obviously wrong in a percentage."},
    {t:"Order of w must match Ω", h:"MMULT will not warn you", b:"The option workbook puts <b>dy first, r<sub>S</sub> second</b>. Reverse w and Excel happily pairs the stock weight with the yield variance, returning a plausible number."},
    {t:"SUMPRODUCT for duration", h:"one cell, and it shows the definition", b:"<code>=SUMPRODUCT(periods, PV_weights)</code>. Macaulay duration <i>is</i> a PV-weighted average time, and this formula makes that impossible to forget."}
  ]
};

/* sorter games: sides = button labels; item.side = index of the right button */
var SORTERS = {
  risks: {
    title: "Which risk is it?",
    prompt: "The deck maps each activity to the risk it generates. Pick the one the lecture would name.",
    sides: ["Market", "Credit", "Operational", "Liquidity"],
    items: [
      {t:"The bank's proprietary desk holds Thai government bonds and the curve steepens.", side:0, why:"<b>Security trading → market risk.</b> Also called price risk or interest rate risk, which is what the whole rest of the course measures."},
      {t:"A corporate borrower misses a scheduled loan repayment.", side:1, why:"<b>Lending → credit risk.</b>"},
      {t:"A settlement clerk mistypes a trade and the error is caught two days later.", side:2, why:"<b>Business process → operational risk.</b> Note the deck's definition of risk explicitly includes mistakes in operations."},
      {t:"Depositors withdraw faster than the bank can sell assets to fund them.", side:3, why:"<b>Managing cash flows associated with obligations → liquidity risk.</b>"},
      {t:"A trading book's VaR model is fine, but 40% of the book is in one issuer.", side:1, why:"This is <b>concentration risk</b>, which the deck lists separately — but its mechanism here is credit: one default would dominate the loss. Concentration is the amplifier, credit is the source."},
      {t:"The equity option desk is short gamma into an earnings announcement.", side:0, why:"<b>Market risk.</b> The exposure is to the underlying price, which lecture 4 measures with Delta and Rho."},
      {t:"A new digital lending strategy turns out to have no profitable customer segment.", side:2, why:"Careful — the deck maps <b>business strategy → strategic risk</b>, not operational. Of the four buttons here operational is nearest, but the deck's own answer is <b>strategic risk</b>, which is its own category."},
      {t:"The bank cannot roll its overnight funding at any price, though all its loans are performing.", side:3, why:"<b>Liquidity risk.</b> Solvency and liquidity are different: every asset can be sound and the institution still fail on timing."},
      {t:"A futures position moves against the desk and margin must be posted today.", side:3, why:"This is the <b>liquidity</b> consequence of a market move. The loss is market risk; the inability to fund the margin call is liquidity risk. Exam questions like this distinction."},
      {t:"An insurer's claims in one year run far above the actuarial expectation.", side:1, why:"None of the four fit — the deck's answer is <b>insurance risk</b>, from selling insurance policies. Credit is the closest button, but the right answer is its own category."}
    ]
  },
  weights: {
    title: "Build the weight vector",
    prompt: "Same master formula every time. What goes in w for this instrument?",
    sides: ["w = share", "w = −D × share", "w = Δ·S/Op", "w in money"],
    items: [
      {t:"100 shares of stock B in a THB 52,772 portfolio.", side:0, why:"A stock's return <i>is</i> the risk factor, so the weight is just its <b>portfolio share</b> — 4,200/52,772 = 7.9587%, with no duration multiplier."},
      {t:"A one-year zero-coupon bond, 20 units, FV 1,000, in the same portfolio.", side:1, why:"<b>w = share × (−D<sub>mod</sub>)</b>. Share 35.30%, D<sub>mod</sub> = 1/(1+y₁) = 0.9314456, so the adjusted weight is −0.3288041."},
      {t:"The fourth coupon of a five-year bond.", side:1, why:"Each cash flow is its own zero. <b>w<sub>4</sub> = (PV₄/W₀) × (−4)</b> under continuous discounting, or × (−4/(1+y₄)) under discrete."},
      {t:"A long put on a stock index, priced with Black-Scholes.", side:2, why:"<b>w = [Δ·S/P ; ρ·1/P]</b>. Two factors, r<sub>S</sub> and dy. On the assignment: −0.0943857 and −0.0977110 after scaling by the option's portfolio share."},
      {t:"A futures contract on a stock, six months to delivery.", side:3, why:"<b>In money: w = [S₀ ; T·S₀].</b> A futures has zero initial value, so there is no share and no percentage return to compute."},
      {t:"A forward contract to buy a share in one year.", side:2, why:"Decompose into long call + short put, then combine the two option weight vectors: <b>w = [S/V<sub>F</sub> ; TKe<sup>−yT</sup>/V<sub>F</sub>]</b>. Still the Δ·S/Op family."},
      {t:"THB 350 held in cash alongside five shares.", side:0, why:"Cash's weight is its <b>share</b> — 50% in the deck's example — and its entire row in Ω is <b>zeros</b>, so it contributes nothing to σ<sub>P</sub>."},
      {t:"A zero-coupon bond futures contract, T = 1, underlying matures at 1.5 years.", side:3, why:"<b>In money: w = [−(T+N) ; +T] × B₀</b> = [−1.387447 ; +0.924964]. Money again, because a futures contract has no initial value to divide by."},
      {t:"A two-year zero inside a bond-plus-stock portfolio worth 2,408.10.", side:1, why:"<b>w = (PV₂/W₀) × (−2)</b> = 41.40767% × (−2) = −0.8281534. Note the share is against the <b>whole portfolio</b>, not against the bond."},
      {t:"The financing leg of a coupon-bond futures contract.", side:3, why:"<b>In money: +T × Σ(X<sub>k</sub>B<sub>k</sub>)</b> — one row shared by every underlying cash flow, and it carries a factor of <b>T</b> that the deck's example drops."}
    ]
  },
  whichfn: {
    title: "Which function?",
    prompt: "One job, two functions that look interchangeable. Pick the one that gives the right answer.",
    sides: ["The first", "The second"],
    items: [
      {t:"You need z for a 99% VaR. <b>NORMSINV(0.01)</b> or <b>NORMSDIST(0.01)</b>?", side:0, why:"You have a probability and want a quantile, so you need the <b>inverse</b>: −2.326348. NORMSDIST(0.01) treats 0.01 as a z-score and returns 0.503989 — a probability, and useless here."},
      {t:"The probability of any loss, given μ and σ. <b>NORMSINV</b> or <b>NORMSDIST</b>?", side:1, why:"<b>NORMSDIST((0−μ)/σ)</b>. You have a value and want a probability, so it is the CDF. The deck's example: NORMSDIST(−0.4) = 0.3446."},
      {t:"σ̂ from 250 daily returns. <b>STDEV</b> or <b>STDEVP</b>?", side:0, why:"<b>STDEV</b> — the n−1 divisor, matching the deck's σ̂² = (1/(T−1))Σ(r−μ̂)². Every class workbook uses it. At n = 250 the gap is only 0.2%, but the convention is what gets marked."},
      {t:"Off-diagonal entries of Ω to match a <b>STDEV^2</b> diagonal. <b>COVAR</b> or <b>COVARIANCE.S</b>?", side:1, why:"<b>COVARIANCE.S</b>, because legacy <code>COVAR</code> is the <b>population</b> version. The class sheets use COVAR and are 0.4% low off the diagonal as a result."},
      {t:"Modified duration for the VaR of a five-year coupon bond. <b>MDURATION</b> or <b>a PV column and SUMPRODUCT</b>?", side:1, why:"<b>Build the column.</b> <code>MDURATION</code> takes a single yield to maturity and real dates; this course discounts every cash flow at its own spot rate over integer periods. MDURATION cannot express that."},
      {t:"d₁ for Black-Scholes. <b>LN(S/K)</b> or <b>LOG(S/K)</b>?", side:0, why:"<b>LN</b> is the natural log. Excel's <code>LOG</code> defaults to base 10 and scales d₁ by ln(10) = 2.3026, silently destroying everything downstream."},
      {t:"N(d₁) in the modern function set. <b>NORM.S.DIST(d1)</b> or <b>NORM.S.DIST(d1,TRUE)</b>?", side:1, why:"The modern function <b>requires the cumulative argument</b>. Without it you get an error; with <code>FALSE</code> you get the density 0.395960 instead of the CDF 0.5714966 — which is how a Gamma ends up in a Delta cell."},
      {t:"σ<sub>P</sub>² from w and Ω. <b>MMULT(MMULT(TRANSPOSE(w),Om),w)</b> or <b>MMULT(w,MMULT(Om,w))</b>?", side:0, why:"<b>The first.</b> w is a column, so the left factor must be transposed into a row; the second form has mismatched dimensions and returns <code>#VALUE!</code>. <code>=SUMPRODUCT(w,MMULT(Om,w))</code> is the shortcut that avoids the issue."},
      {t:"The normal density inside Vega. <b>SQRT(2*22/7)</b> or <b>SQRT(2*PI())</b>?", side:1, why:"<b>PI().</b> 22/7 = 3.142857 is not π, and since it sits in the denominator the density — and Vega and Gamma with it — comes out 0.02012% too small."},
      {t:"Macaulay duration from a period column and a weight column. <b>SUMPRODUCT</b> or <b>SUM</b>?", side:0, why:"<b>SUMPRODUCT(periods, weights)</b>. It multiplies pairwise then adds, which is the definition of a PV-weighted average time. <code>SUM</code> would just total the weights to 1."},
      {t:"A binomial node payoff. <b>MAX(0, K−S)</b> or <b>ABS(K−S)</b>?", side:0, why:"<b>MAX(0, …)</b>. An option's payoff is floored at zero, not reflected — <code>ABS</code> would pay out when the put is out of the money."},
      {t:"Annualising a daily σ for Black-Scholes. <b>σ×SQRT(252)</b> or <b>σ×252</b>?", side:0, why:"<b>√252.</b> Volatility scales with the square root of time; only the variance scales linearly. The class sheet writes <code>=K7*SQRT(252)</code>."}
    ]
  },
  breaks: {
    title: "Will this formula break?",
    prompt: "Some of these return an error. Others return a wrong number and say nothing, which is worse.",
    sides: ["Errors visibly", "Wrong, but silent"],
    items: [
      {t:"<b>=MMULT(MMULT(TRANSPOSE(w),Om),w)</b> entered with plain Enter in Excel 2019", side:1, why:"Returns the <b>top-left element</b> — w₁²Ω₁₁ instead of wᵀΩw. No error. For a diversified book the result is far too small, which is the classic symptom of a missing <b>Ctrl+Shift+Enter</b>."},
      {t:"<b>=MMULT(w, Om)</b> with w as a 3×1 column and Ω as 3×3", side:0, why:"<b>#VALUE!</b> The columns of the first array (1) must equal the rows of the second (3). You needed <code>TRANSPOSE(w)</code>."},
      {t:"<b>=(B5-B4)</b> instead of <b>=(B5-B4)/100</b> for a yield change", side:1, why:"Perfectly valid arithmetic. Your dy is in <b>percentage points</b> instead of decimal, so every bond VaR comes out <b>100× too large</b> — and in a percentage output that can still look superficially plausible."},
      {t:"<b>=NORMSDIST(0.01)</b> where you meant NORMSINV", side:1, why:"Returns 0.503989. A number between 0 and 1, no error, and a VaR built on it is nonsense."},
      {t:"<b>=NORM.S.DIST(d1)</b> without the cumulative argument", side:0, why:"<b>Errors.</b> The modern function requires the second argument. This is one case where the strictness helps you — the legacy <code>NORMSDIST(d1)</code> would just work."},
      {t:"<b>=SQRT(MMULT(MMULT(TRANSPOSE(w),Om),w))</b> on a Ω that is not positive semi-definite", side:0, why:"<b>#NUM!</b> from SQRT, because the quadratic form came out negative. Unpleasant to debug, but it is the only warning you get that the matrix is impossible — which can happen when you mix n and n−1 conventions."},
      {t:"Black-Scholes with σ = 5% per month and T = 1 (meaning one year)", side:1, why:"Returns a price. The wrong price, because σ and T disagree about what a period is. Nothing in Excel can detect this — only you can."},
      {t:"<b>=EXP(-(d^2)/2)/SQRT(2*22/7)</b> for the density", side:1, why:"Returns a perfectly good number, 0.02012% too small. It propagates into Vega and Gamma only, so option prices and every VaR in this course are unaffected."},
      {t:"Reversing w to [r<sub>S</sub> ; dy] while Ω is still ordered [dy ; r<sub>S</sub>]", side:1, why:"<b>MMULT does not check labels.</b> It pairs the stock weight with the yield variance and returns a plausible σ<sub>P</sub>. On the option example that turns 7.92% into something quite different, with no warning."},
      {t:"<b>=varcovar(F5:H255)</b> in a blank workbook", side:0, why:"<b>#NAME?</b> — it is a <b>VBA user-defined function</b> from the course files, not standard Excel. Open the .xlsm with macros enabled, or rebuild Ω with COVARIANCE.S."},
      {t:"<b>=MDURATION(5, 10, 0.04, 0.05, 2)</b> meaning five years to maturity", side:0, why:"<b>#NUM!</b> or nonsense. Excel reads 5 and 10 as <b>date serial numbers</b> — 5 and 10 January 1900 — so maturity is five days after settlement."},
      {t:"Multiplying a one-day VaR that contains μ by <b>SQRT(3)</b>", side:1, why:"Returns a number. The wrong one: it scales the <b>mean</b> by √3 when the mean should scale by <b>3</b>. On the portfolio example that is −2.51503% instead of −2.54849%."},
      {t:"Computing a percentage return for a futures contract as <b>dFu/Fu₀</b>", side:0, why:"<b>#DIV/0!</b> — the contract's initial value is <b>zero</b>. Excel catches this one, and the error is the lesson: futures VaR must be in money."},
      {t:"Including a coupon paid <b>before</b> delivery in a futures price", side:1, why:"Arithmetic runs fine and gives a futures price that is too high. The check that catches it: the price must sit <b>between the PV of the post-delivery flows and their undiscounted sum</b>."}
    ]
  }
};
